{"id":"public-financing","title":"Public campaign money: what each candidate received, what came back and what the audits are known to have found","question":"How much public money did each candidate for Mayor or Supervisor receive since 2014, how much was returned, which committees were later named in a penalty, and what do the Ethics Commission's audits say?","finding":"Campaign statements show $21.7 million of public funds received by 90 candidate committees for elections from 2014 to 2026, $8.6 million of it by 26 committees for 2024; the Ethics Commission reports $8.8 million approved for 27 candidates that year. 26 committees reported returning $246,104 to the City, and 10 are named as respondents in a later penalty on the Commission's list ($125,780 in all, for any violation). The Commission says it was unable to complete the required audits of publicly financed candidates for the 2020 and 2022 elections, and that all 27 audits for 2024 were delivered about a year after the election. The audit reports themselves are not in the document folder, so their findings (disallowed claims, repayments owed) cannot be tabulated.","how":"Public funds are the receipts candidate committees report on Schedule I of the campaign statement as paid by the City and County, the Ethics Commission or the Controller and described as public financing or matching funds. Private money is every contribution on Schedule A and C. Returned is a payment to the City on the spending schedule described as a return of public or unexpended funds. The election year is read from the committee's name. A penalty is shown only where the respondents in the Commission's penalty file include this committee by name, dated in or after the election year; it is for any violation, not only public-financing rules, and penalties on the candidate personally or on another of the candidate's committees are not attached. The statements about audits are quoted from the Commission's annual report for fiscal 2025 and its director's report of September 2026.","limits":"Public financing is the City's own programme; a high public share means a campaign with many small local donors. The figures are what committees typed on their statements, with descriptions that vary, so a payment described unusually can be missed or a refund miscounted: the 2024 total from the filings differs from the Commission's by $217,552. Most audit findings in this field are bookkeeping, and most of the penalties shown are for disclaimers and reporting. Nothing here shows a disallowed matching claim, because that is recorded only in the audit reports.","tier":"OFFICIAL","columns":[{"k":"committee","t":"Candidate committee","type":"text"},{"k":"year","t":"Election","type":"year"},{"k":"public","t":"Public funds received","type":"usd"},{"k":"private","t":"Private contributions","type":"usd"},{"k":"publicShare","t":"Public share","type":"pct"},{"k":"payments","t":"Public payments","type":"int"},{"k":"returned","t":"Returned to the City","type":"usd"},{"k":"forfeited","t":"Over-limit contributions forfeited to the City","type":"usd"},{"k":"penalty","t":"Penalties in cases naming this committee","type":"usd"},{"k":"penaltyCase","t":"Case","type":"text"}],"rows":[{"committee":"Mark Farrell for Mayor 2024","year":2024,"public":1200000,"private":975522,"publicShare":0.552,"payments":6,"returned":null,"forfeited":null,"penalty":108180,"penaltyCase":"24-000817 (2024)"},{"committee":"Aaron Peskin for Mayor 2024","year":2024,"public":1200000,"private":924463,"publicShare":0.565,"payments":5,"returned":null,"forfeited":6357,"penalty":null,"penaltyCase":null},{"committee":"Re-Elect Mayor London Breed 2024","year":2024,"public":1185000,"private":1183679,"publicShare":0.5,"payments":7,"returned":268,"forfeited":null,"penalty":null,"penaltyCase":null},{"committee":"Mark Leno for Mayor 2018","year":2018,"public":975000,"private":942372,"publicShare":0.509,"payments":7,"returned":20120,"forfeited":null,"penalty":1000,"penalty